    "\"FINAL_ANSWER: {:result \\\"{:scaled-ingredients \\\\\\\"*   **Vegetable Base:** 12kg Eggplant (large dice), 10kg Zucchini (medium dice), 8kg Yellow Squash (medium dice), 10kg Red/Yellow Bell Peppers (1-inch pieces).\\\\\\\\n*   **Aromatics:** 6kg Yellow Onions (small dice), 150g Garlic (minced), 4kg Roma Tomatoes (concassé) + 6 liters high-quality Tomato Passata.\\\\\\\\n*   **Fats & Seasoning:** 3 Liters Extra Virgin Olive Oil, 250g Kosher Salt (added in stages), 60g Cracked Black Pepper, 100g Herbes de Provence.\\\\\\\\n*   **Finish:** 500g Fresh Basil (chiffonade, added at service), 100ml Sherry Vinegar (to brighten acidity).\\\\\\\\n*   *Note: Scaling is adjusted to 0.85x for seasoning to prevent over-salting during reduction.*\\\\\\\", :revised-technique \\\\\\\"For 60 portions, traditional one-pot stovetop methods lead to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"stewing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" rather than \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sautéing,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" resulting in a mushy grey texture. \\\\\\\\n1. **Component Roasting:** Utilize industrial convection ovens. Toss eggplant, peppers, and squash in oil/salt and roast on sheet pans at 220°C (425°F) until caramelized and al dente. This preserves individual cell structure.\\\\\\\\n2. **The Base:** Build a concentrated \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Piperade\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (onion, garlic, tomato, herbs) in a large tilting skillet or heavy-bottomed stockpot. \\\\\\\\n3. **The Marriage:** Fold the roasted vegetables into the sauce base for the final 15 minutes of cooking to allow flavors to meld without breaking down the vegetable shapes.\\\\\\\", :workflow \\\\\\\"1. **Prep (Day 1 or Morning of):** Mechanical dicing (if available) or organized station prep for all 40kg+ of vegetables. Keep eggplant separate as it oxidizes.\\\\\\\\n2. **Roasting Phase:** Stagger sheet pans in convection ovens. Rotate every 10 minutes. \\\\\\\\n3. **Sauce Phase:** Sauté onions and peppers in the tilt skillet. Add aromatics and tomato products. Simmer to reduce water content by 20%.\\\\\\\\n4. **Assembly:** Combine roasted components into the sauce.\\\\\\\\n5. **Holding:** Transfer to shallow 2-inch hotel pans. Do not stack deep, as the weight will crush the bottom layer.\\\\\\\\n6. **Service:** Garnish with fresh basil and a drizzle of raw EVOO immediately before plating.\\\\\\\", :timing \\\\\\\"*   **Mise en Place:** 3 hours (2 chefs) or 5 hours (1 chef).\\\\\\\\n*   **Roasting/Caramelization:** 1.5 hours (staggered batches).\\\\\\\\n*   **Sauce Reduction:** 45 minutes.\\\\\\\\n*   **Meld/Final Simmer:** 20 minutes.\\\\\\\\n*   **Total Active Time:** ~6 hours.\\\\\\\", :cost-estimate \\\\\\\"*   **Produce:** $140 - $180 (seasonal fluctuations heavily impact bell pepper and zucchini prices).\\\\\\\\n*   **Pantry (Oil/Herbs/Vinegar):** $40 - $50.\\\\\\\\n*   **Total Estimated Cost:** $180 - $230.\\\\\\\\n*   **Cost Per Plate:** $3.00 - $3.85.\\\\\\\", :pitfalls-and-mitigations \\\\\\\"*   **Pitfall: The \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Mush\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" Factor.** Scaling 10x in one pot creates too much steam, boiling the vegetables in their own juice. \\\\\\\\n    *   *Mitigation:* Use the sheet-pan roasting method described in 'Revised Technique'.\\\\\\\\n*   **Pitfall: Over-salting.** Salt does not scale linearly; 10x salt in a reduced sauce is often inedible. \\\\\\\\n    *   *Mitigation:* Salt the roasting vegetables lightly; season the sauce base to 80% of desired saltiness, then perform a final adjustment after combining.\\\\\\\\n*   **Pitfall: Eggplant Bitterness.** Large batches of eggplant can release excess bitter liquid.\\\\\\\\n    *   *Mitigation:* Pre-salt the diced eggplant in colanders for 30 minutes and pat dry before roasting.\\\\\\\\n*   **Pitfall: Thermal Carryover.** 60 portions of hot vegetables hold massive residual heat. \\\\\\\\n    *   *Mitigation:* If not serving immediately, spread in shallow pans and blast-chill to stop the cooking process, then reheat gently for service.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}\"",
